<!-- {{ ansible_managed }} -->
<!--
  The public context — everything arriving from the Kamailio edge.

  THIS DIALPLAN MAKES NO ROUTING DECISIONS. That is the design: FreeSWITCH is a
  dumb B2BUA, and every call hands control to the ESL controller, which decides
  what happens next. Putting routing logic here would mean two places that
  decide where calls go, drifting apart, one of them requiring an Ansible run
  to change.
-->
<include>
  <context name="public">

    <extension name="intron_inbound">
      <condition field="destination_number" expression="^(.*)$">

        <!--
          Answer semantics, in order, and the order matters.

          pre_answer rather than answer: the call is not answered until the
          controller says to. Answering first would start billing on the
          carrier leg for a call that may turn out to be unroutable, and would
          make a rejection look to the caller like a call that connected and
          then died.
        -->
        <action application="set" data="hangup_after_bridge=true"/>
        <action application="set" data="continue_on_fail=false"/>

        <!--
          intron_tenant and intron_plan USED TO BE SET HERE, from the
          X-Intron-Tenant / X-Intron-Plan headers Kamailio appended after its
          admission lookup. Both are deleted because Kamailio no longer makes
          that lookup or appends those headers — the controller resolves tenant
          and plan itself, in-process, once it has the call (docs/API.md,
          Contract 1).

          DELETED RATHER THAN LEFT TO RESOLVE EMPTY, and that is the point.
          `set` against a missing header does not fail; it produces a variable
          that exists and is "". Code reading intron_tenant would get an empty
          string, and an empty tenant scopes every query to nothing — no error
          anywhere, just a call behaving as though the tenant has no
          configuration.

          intron_node stays: it is this node's own name, not something a header
          carried.
        -->
        <action application="set" data="intron_node={{ inventory_hostname }}"/>

        <!--
          ===================================================================
          THE NEXT HOP FOR EVERY LEG THIS CALL BRIDGES OUT TO.

          Without this, a bridged B-leg goes straight to whatever its
          Request-URI resolves to — for a handoff, the client's SBC; for a
          carrier destination, the carrier — BYPASSING KAMAILIO AND RTPENGINE
          entirely. The call sets up cleanly and has no audio, because the SDP
          then carries this node's private address and nothing rewrote it.
          For a carrier it fails differently and more loudly: the INVITE
          leaves via the NAT gateway, whose address is not in Twilio's
          Termination IP ACL, and Twilio answers 403.

          `nolocal:` puts the variable on the OUTBOUND leg only. Without the
          prefix it would also land on this inbound channel, where a next-hop
          override has no meaning and could affect how in-dialog requests are
          sent back toward Kamailio.

          WHY HERE AND NOT IN THE CONTROLLER'S DIAL STRING. Which edge this
          node sends through is a fact about THIS FreeSWITCH node, and Ansible
          already knows it — edge_private_ips comes from the inventory. Putting
          a second copy in the controller's environment would be one address in
          two places with nothing keeping them in step.

          THIS DOES NOT COVER OUTBOUND ORIGINATE. An originate has no inbound
          A-leg, so no dialplan runs and this export never executes — the
          controller must carry sip_route_uri in the originate dial string
          itself (docs/API.md, Contract 3). The two paths are not redundant;
          they are different paths.

          Verify it lands on the B-leg the first time a call bridges:
            fs_cli -x "uuid_dump <b-leg-uuid>" | grep sip_route_uri
          ===================================================================
        -->
        <action application="export"
                data="nolocal:sip_route_uri=sip:{{ edge_private_ips[0] }}:{{ sip_port }}"/>

        <!--
          ===================================================================
          ARROW 3 IN THE ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAM.

          FreeSWITCH opens a TCP connection to the ESL controller and hands it
          the call. `async full` means the controller gets the full event
          stream and can issue commands, and the socket stays open FOR THE LIFE
          OF THE CALL.

          That last part is what every deploy decision in the control-plane
          stack is built around: the NLB's 3600s deregistration delay, the
          raised TCP idle timeout, the drain in the systemd ExecStop, and the
          two app instances that make draining possible at all.

          THE TARGET IS A DNS NAME, NEVER AN ADDRESS. A Route53 record in the
          private zone, pointing at the internal NLB. That indirection is what
          makes the controller replaceable without touching a single
          FreeSWITCH node.

          IF THIS VALUE IS WRONG, EVERY INBOUND CALL DROPS AT ANSWER, and the
          controller's log shows nothing at all — because it was never reached.
          The freeswitch role checks reachability at the end of the play for
          exactly this reason.
          ===================================================================
        -->
        <action application="socket" data="{{ freeswitch_esl_outbound_target }} async full"/>

        <!--
          Reached only if the socket application returns without the controller
          having bridged or hung up the call — which means the controller
          accepted the connection and then failed. 503 rather than a silent
          hangup so the carrier sees a cause code and it appears in the CDR as
          a platform failure rather than as a normal clearing.
        -->
        <action application="log" data="ERR controller returned without handling call uuid=${uuid} did=${destination_number}"/>
        <action application="hangup" data="NORMAL_TEMPORARY_FAILURE"/>

      </condition>
    </extension>

  </context>
</include>
